1
00:00:02,914 --> 00:00:04,897
More details
about the new <i>Star Trek</i> film.

2
00:00:05,356 --> 00:00:08,746
There's going to be a scene
depicting Spock's birth.

3
00:00:09,394 --> 00:00:12,321
I'd be more interested in a scene
depicting Spock's conception.

4
00:00:13,690 --> 00:00:14,628
Oh, please.

5
00:00:14,748 --> 00:00:18,123
For Vulcans, mating...
or if you will, <i>Pon farr</i>...

6
00:00:19,338 --> 00:00:22,851
- it's an extremely private matter.
- Still, I'd like to know the details.

7
00:00:22,971 --> 00:00:24,896
His mother was human.
His father is Vulcan.

8
00:00:25,016 --> 00:00:27,681
- They couldn't just conceive.
- Maybe they had to go to a clinic.

9
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:29,793
Can you imagine Spock's Dad
in a little room

10
00:00:29,913 --> 00:00:32,448
with a copy of
<i>Pointy Ears and Shapely Rears</i>.

11
00:00:34,113 --> 00:00:36,678
How come on <i>Star Trek</i>
everybody's private parts are the same?

12
00:00:37,561 --> 00:00:41,382
No alien lady ever told Captain Kirk,
"Hey, get your thing out of my nose."

13
00:00:44,360 --> 00:00:45,586
Hi. Can you help me?

14
00:00:45,706 --> 00:00:47,968
I was writing an e-mail
and the "A" key got stuck.

15
00:00:48,088 --> 00:00:49,809
Now it's just going "Aaa..."

16
00:00:50,936 --> 00:00:53,112
- What'd you spill on it?
- Nothing.

17
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:55,154
Diet Coke.

18
00:00:57,004 --> 00:00:58,051
And yogurt.

19
00:00:58,761 --> 00:01:00,118
And a little nail polish.

20
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:01,745
I'll take a look at it.

21
00:01:02,175 --> 00:01:04,979
Gentlemen,
switching to local nerd news...

22
00:01:05,306 --> 00:01:08,006
Fishman, Chen, Chaudury and McNair
aren't fielding a team

23
00:01:08,126 --> 00:01:10,003
in the university Physics Bowl
this year.

24
00:01:10,163 --> 00:01:11,504
You're kidding. Why not?

25
00:01:11,665 --> 00:01:14,101
They formed a barbershop quartet
and got a gig

26
00:01:14,221 --> 00:01:15,994
playing Knott's Berry Farm.

27
00:01:17,644 --> 00:01:19,083
So, in your world,

28
00:01:19,626 --> 00:01:21,311
you're like the cool guys.

29
00:01:22,575 --> 00:01:23,734
Recognize.

30
00:01:25,971 --> 00:01:28,170
This is our year.
With those guys out,

31
00:01:28,290 --> 00:01:30,398
the entire Physics Bowl
will kneel before Zod.

32
00:01:31,052 --> 00:01:31,896
Zod?

33
00:01:32,103 --> 00:01:33,735
Kryptonian villain. Long story.

34
00:01:33,895 --> 00:01:34,936
Good story.

35
00:01:38,567 --> 00:01:40,825
- Count me out.
- What? Why?

36
00:01:41,194 --> 00:01:44,204
You want me to use my intelligence
in a tawdry competition?

37
00:01:44,844 --> 00:01:47,237
Would you ask Picasso
to play Pictionary?

38
00:01:48,315 --> 00:01:50,745
Would you ask Noah Webster
to play Boggle?

39
00:01:51,707 --> 00:01:54,408
Would you ask Jacques Cousteau
to play Go Fish?

40
00:01:55,876 --> 00:01:58,889
Come on, you need a four-person team.
We're four people.

41
00:01:59,009 --> 00:02:02,137
By that reasoning we should also
play Bridge, hold up a chuppah

42
00:02:02,257 --> 00:02:04,687
and enter the Olympic
bobsled competition.

43
00:02:05,503 --> 00:02:07,102
Tickets to that, please.

44
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:12,023
Sheldon, what, do I need to quote
Spock's dying words to you?

45
00:02:12,184 --> 00:02:13,317
No, don't.

46
00:02:14,266 --> 00:02:15,903
"The needs of the many...

47
00:02:16,063 --> 00:02:18,070
"... outweigh the needs of the few.

48
00:02:18,232 --> 00:02:19,294
"Or the one."

49
00:02:20,349 --> 00:02:21,786
Damn it, I'll do it.

50
00:02:23,471 --> 00:02:25,901
The Big Bang Theory
Season 1 Episode 13 - The Bat Jar Conjecture

51
00:02:26,490 --> 00:02:28,985
Transcript: swsub.com
Sync: SerJo

52
00:02:48,925 --> 00:02:50,688
First order of Physics Bowl business.

53
00:02:51,511 --> 00:02:54,900
We need a truly kick-ass team name.
Suggestions?

54
00:02:55,266 --> 00:02:57,677
How about the Perpetual Motion Squad?

55
00:02:58,883 --> 00:03:02,742
It's beyond the laws of physics,
plus a little heads-up for the ladies.

56
00:03:03,510 --> 00:03:04,368
The ladies?

57
00:03:04,529 --> 00:03:07,604
"Perpetual Motion Squad...
we can go all night."

58
00:03:08,532 --> 00:03:10,326
- I like it.
- I don't.

59
00:03:10,837 --> 00:03:13,323
Teams are traditionally
named after fierce creatures,

60
00:03:13,443 --> 00:03:15,462
thus intimidating one's opponent.

61
00:03:15,623 --> 00:03:18,333
- Then we could be the Bengal tigers.
- Poor choice.

62
00:03:18,453 --> 00:03:20,503
Gram for gram,
no animal exceeds

63
00:03:20,623 --> 00:03:23,240
the relative fighting strength
of the army ant.

64
00:03:24,130 --> 00:03:25,121
Maybe so,

65
00:03:25,241 --> 00:03:28,351
but you can't incinerate a Bengal tiger
with a magnifying glass.

66
00:03:31,938 --> 00:03:33,926
Let's put it to a vote.
All those in favor...

67
00:03:34,046 --> 00:03:35,065
Point of order.

68
00:03:35,226 --> 00:03:37,734
I move that any vote on team names
must be unanimous.

69
00:03:37,854 --> 00:03:40,500
No man should be forced to emblazon
his chest with a Bengal tiger

70
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:43,392
when common sense dictates
it should be an army ant.

71
00:03:44,235 --> 00:03:47,396
Will the gentleman from the great
State of Denial yield for a question?

72
00:03:48,219 --> 00:03:49,172
I will yield.

73
00:03:49,292 --> 00:03:52,175
After we go through the exercise
of an annoying series of votes,

74
00:03:52,295 --> 00:03:55,326
all of which the gentleman will lose,
does he then intend to threaten to quit

75
00:03:55,446 --> 00:03:57,278
- if he does not get his way?
- He does.

76
00:03:57,398 --> 00:03:59,764
I move we are the Army Ants.
All those in favor?

77
00:04:01,899 --> 00:04:05,388
Good afternoon, and welcome
to today's Physics Bowl practice round.

78
00:04:05,549 --> 00:04:07,237
I'm Penny,
and I'll be your host

79
00:04:07,357 --> 00:04:09,574
because apparently
I didn't have anything else to do

80
00:04:09,694 --> 00:04:12,965
on a Saturday afternoon,
and isn't that just a little sad?

81
00:04:15,015 --> 00:04:16,147
Gentlemen, ready?

82
00:04:16,308 --> 00:04:17,692
- Yes.
- Fire away.

83
00:04:17,812 --> 00:04:19,837
It's none of my business,
but isn't a guy

84
00:04:19,957 --> 00:04:22,564
who can't speak in front of women
gonna hold you back a little?

85
00:04:22,684 --> 00:04:25,256
He'll be okay once the women
are mixed into the crowd.

86
00:04:25,376 --> 00:04:28,636
He only has a problem
when they're one-on-one and smell nice.

87
00:04:29,455 --> 00:04:31,330
Thanks, Raj. It's vanilla oil.

88
00:04:31,717 --> 00:04:34,667
I was actually the one who noticed.
Okay, let's just start.

89
00:04:35,262 --> 00:04:38,112
Okay, the first question
is on the topic of optics.

90
00:04:38,232 --> 00:04:40,381
"What is the shortest
light pulse ever produced?"

91
00:04:41,333 --> 00:04:42,900
- Dr. Cooper.
- And of course,

92
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:44,719
the answer is 130 attoseconds.

93
00:04:44,986 --> 00:04:46,929
- That is correct.
- I knew that, too.

94
00:04:47,747 --> 00:04:50,665
Good for you, sweetie.
Okay, next question:

95
00:04:50,785 --> 00:04:52,579
"What is the quantum mechanical effect

96
00:04:52,699 --> 00:04:54,812
"used to encode data
on hard-disk drives?"

97
00:04:56,531 --> 00:04:58,982
And of course the answer is
giant magnetoresistance.

98
00:04:59,218 --> 00:05:01,000
- Right.
- Hey, I buzzed in.

99
00:05:01,453 --> 00:05:03,621
And I answered.
It's called teamwork.

100
00:05:05,608 --> 00:05:08,089
Don't you think I should answer
the engineering questions?

101
00:05:08,209 --> 00:05:09,397
I am an engineer.

102
00:05:09,701 --> 00:05:12,223
By that logic I should answer
all the anthropology questions

103
00:05:12,343 --> 00:05:13,774
because I'm a mammal.

104
00:05:16,719 --> 00:05:18,002
Just ask another one.

105
00:05:19,529 --> 00:05:21,727
"What artificial satellite
has seen glimpses

106
00:05:21,847 --> 00:05:23,799
of Einstein's predicted
frame dragging?"

107
00:05:24,843 --> 00:05:27,059
And of course,
it's Gravity Probe B.

108
00:05:27,658 --> 00:05:30,221
Sheldon, you have to let
somebody else answer.

109
00:05:30,548 --> 00:05:31,257
Why?

110
00:05:31,510 --> 00:05:32,682
Because it's polite.

111
00:05:33,266 --> 00:05:35,284
What do manners
have to do with it?

112
00:05:35,444 --> 00:05:36,596
This is war.

113
00:05:37,544 --> 00:05:40,115
Were the Romans polite
when they salted the ground of Carthage

114
00:05:40,275 --> 00:05:42,267
to make sure
nothing would ever grow again?

115
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:45,831
Leonard, you said
I only had to ask questions.

116
00:05:47,941 --> 00:05:50,388
The objective of the competion
is to give correct answers.

117
00:05:50,548 --> 00:05:52,533
If I know them,
why shouldn't I give them?

118
00:05:52,693 --> 00:05:55,166
Some of us might have
the correct answers, too.

119
00:05:55,416 --> 00:05:57,441
Oh, please.
You don't even have a PhD.

120
00:05:57,601 --> 00:05:59,836
- All right, that's it!
- Howard, sit down.

121
00:06:02,159 --> 00:06:05,239
- Maybe we should take a little break.
- Good idea. I need my wrist brace.

122
00:06:05,399 --> 00:06:08,544
All this button-pushing
is aggravating my old Nintendo injury.

123
00:06:10,047 --> 00:06:10,887
I agree.

124
00:06:11,250 --> 00:06:12,567
What did he say?

125
00:06:12,955 --> 00:06:14,271
He compared Sheldon

126
00:06:14,391 --> 00:06:17,510
to a disposable feminine
cleansing product

127
00:06:17,670 --> 00:06:19,768
one might use
on a summer's eve.

128
00:06:22,359 --> 00:06:24,133
Yeah, and the bag it came in.

129
00:06:28,743 --> 00:06:30,907
Leonard, excellent.
I want to show you something.

130
00:06:31,253 --> 00:06:32,924
Can it wait?
I need to talk to you.

131
00:06:33,113 --> 00:06:36,422
Just look. I've designed
the perfect uniforms for our team.

132
00:06:36,582 --> 00:06:38,932
The colors are based
on <i>Star Trek: The Original Series</i>.

133
00:06:39,168 --> 00:06:42,486
The three of you will wear Support Red,
and I will wear Command Gold.

134
00:06:44,496 --> 00:06:45,790
Why do they say "AA"?

135
00:06:46,551 --> 00:06:47,549
Army Ants.

136
00:06:49,054 --> 00:06:52,713
Isn't that confusing? "AA" might mean
something else to certain people.

137
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:56,879
Why would a Physics Bowl team
be called Anodized Aluminum?

138
00:06:58,091 --> 00:06:59,253
No, I meant...

139
00:07:00,379 --> 00:07:01,379
Never mind.

140
00:07:02,479 --> 00:07:03,479
Check it out,

141
00:07:04,082 --> 00:07:07,039
I got you a <i>Batman</i> cookie jar.

142
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:10,182
Oh, neat!
What's the occasion?

143
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,796
Well, you're a friend,
and you like <i>Batman</i>

144
00:07:12,956 --> 00:07:15,152
and cookies,
and you're off the team.

145
00:07:18,861 --> 00:07:19,770
What?

146
00:07:20,213 --> 00:07:22,365
Howard, Raj and I
just had a team meeting.

147
00:07:22,582 --> 00:07:25,058
- No, you didn't.
- Yes, we did. I just came from there.

148
00:07:25,178 --> 00:07:27,259
Okay, I don't know
where you just came from,

149
00:07:27,419 --> 00:07:30,425
but it could'nt have been a team meeting
because I wasn't there.

150
00:07:30,591 --> 00:07:32,394
Ergo, the team did not meet.

151
00:07:33,263 --> 00:07:35,255
Okay, let me try it this way:

152
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:38,916
I was at a coffee klatch
with a couple of friends,

153
00:07:39,076 --> 00:07:42,323
and one thing led to another,
and it turns out you're off the team.

154
00:07:43,592 --> 00:07:44,401
Why?

155
00:07:44,601 --> 00:07:46,684
Because you're taking
all the fun out of it.

156
00:07:47,087 --> 00:07:50,844
I'm sorry, is the winner of the Physics
Bowl the team that has the most fun?

157
00:07:51,073 --> 00:07:52,734
Okay, let me try it this way:

158
00:07:52,894 --> 00:07:56,107
You're annoying and no one
wants to play with you anymore.

159
00:07:58,400 --> 00:07:59,352
I see.

160
00:08:01,622 --> 00:08:03,784
At this point I should inform you

161
00:08:04,221 --> 00:08:06,461
that I intend to form my own team

162
00:08:06,621 --> 00:08:09,962
and destroy the molecular bonds
that bind your very matter together

163
00:08:10,122 --> 00:08:13,103
and reduce the resulting
particulate chaos to tears.

164
00:08:15,934 --> 00:08:17,548
Thanks for the heads-up.

165
00:08:18,338 --> 00:08:19,307
You're welcome.

166
00:08:21,095 --> 00:08:22,478
- One more thing.
- Yes?

167
00:08:22,688 --> 00:08:23,917
It's on, bitch.

168
00:08:33,958 --> 00:08:36,774
- So who'd he get to be on his team?
- He won't say.

169
00:08:37,265 --> 00:08:40,293
He just smiles and eats
macaroons out of his bat jar.

170
00:08:41,710 --> 00:08:43,330
He's using psychological warfare.

171
00:08:43,776 --> 00:08:47,281
We must reply in kind.
I say we wait until he looks at us,

172
00:08:47,441 --> 00:08:50,979
then laugh like, "Yes, you are
a smart and strong competitor,

173
00:08:51,139 --> 00:08:52,928
"but we are also smart and strong,

174
00:08:53,088 --> 00:08:55,779
and we have a reasonable
chance of defeating you."

175
00:08:57,791 --> 00:08:59,547
How exactly would that laugh go?

176
00:09:04,841 --> 00:09:07,739
That sounds more like,
"We are a tall, thin woman

177
00:09:07,899 --> 00:09:10,438
who wants to make a
coat out of your Dalmatians."

178
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:16,154
Let's remember that Sheldon
is still our friend and my roommate.

179
00:09:16,407 --> 00:09:17,207
So?

180
00:09:18,112 --> 00:09:19,984
So nothing.
Let's destroy him.

181
00:09:21,371 --> 00:09:22,371
Gentlemen.

182
00:09:30,644 --> 00:09:32,914
We're going to need
a strong fourth for our team.

183
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:36,876
You know who is apparently very smart
is the girl who played <i>TV's Blossom</i>.

184
00:09:38,839 --> 00:09:40,887
She got a PhD. in neuroscience
or something.

185
00:09:41,067 --> 00:09:44,894
Raj, we're not getting <i>TV's Blossom</i>
to join our Physics Bowl team.

186
00:09:47,068 --> 00:09:49,180
How about the girl
from <i>The Wonder Years</i>?

187
00:09:50,928 --> 00:09:54,561
Gentlemen, I believe I've found
the solution to all our problems.

188
00:09:56,981 --> 00:09:58,677
We can't ask Leslie Winkle.

189
00:09:58,969 --> 00:10:01,654
Why? Because you slept together,
and when she was done with you

190
00:10:01,814 --> 00:10:04,302
she discarded you
like last night's chutney?

191
00:10:08,387 --> 00:10:09,247
Yes.

192
00:10:10,411 --> 00:10:12,467
Sometimes you've got
to take one for the team.

193
00:10:12,859 --> 00:10:14,186
Sack up, dude.

194
00:10:15,999 --> 00:10:16,803
Fine.

195
00:10:18,486 --> 00:10:20,857
Here I go, taking one for the team...

196
00:10:21,198 --> 00:10:22,301
in the sack.

197
00:10:25,535 --> 00:10:27,726
- Hey, Leslie.
- Hi, guys.

198
00:10:28,069 --> 00:10:30,270
So, Leslie,
I have a question for you,

199
00:10:30,432 --> 00:10:34,403
and it might be a little awkward,
you know, given that I...

200
00:10:34,781 --> 00:10:35,940
Hit that thing.

201
00:10:39,525 --> 00:10:41,739
Leonard, there's no reason
to feel uncomfortable

202
00:10:41,859 --> 00:10:43,781
just because we've seen
each other's faces

203
00:10:43,901 --> 00:10:46,955
and naked bodies contorted
in the sweet agony of coitus.

204
00:10:48,434 --> 00:10:49,425
There's not?

205
00:10:49,545 --> 00:10:52,117
Gee, 'cause it sure sounds
like there should be.

206
00:10:53,008 --> 00:10:55,624
Rest assured that any aspects
of our sexual relationship

207
00:10:55,744 --> 00:10:59,083
regarding your preferences,
your idiosyncrasies, your performance

208
00:10:59,203 --> 00:11:02,111
are still protected by the inherent
confidentiality of the bedroom.

209
00:11:02,231 --> 00:11:04,074
That's all very comforting,
but if it's okay,

210
00:11:04,194 --> 00:11:06,184
I'd like to get on
to my question now.

211
00:11:06,785 --> 00:11:07,593
Proceed.

212
00:11:08,028 --> 00:11:11,467
We are entering the Physics Bowl,
and we need a fourth for our team.

213
00:11:11,587 --> 00:11:15,108
No, thanks. I'm really busy with my
like-sign dilepton supersymmetry search.

214
00:11:15,268 --> 00:11:17,487
Dilepton, shmylepton.
We need you.

215
00:11:18,956 --> 00:11:19,816
Sorry.

216
00:11:21,087 --> 00:11:22,507
Well, we tried.

217
00:11:22,863 --> 00:11:26,412
We'll just have to face Sheldon
<i>mano y mano y mano a mano</i>.

218
00:11:27,323 --> 00:11:29,580
Wait, you're going up
against Sheldon Cooper?

219
00:11:30,783 --> 00:11:33,684
That arrogant, misogynistic,
East Texas doorknob

220
00:11:33,804 --> 00:11:36,499
that told me I should abandon
my work with high-energy particles

221
00:11:36,619 --> 00:11:38,880
for laundry and childbearing?

222
00:11:41,917 --> 00:11:42,964
She's in.

223
00:11:45,870 --> 00:11:47,366
So, how do you feel?

224
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:50,852
Nice and loose? Come to play?
Got your game face on?

225
00:11:52,305 --> 00:11:53,478
Are you ready?

226
00:11:54,348 --> 00:11:56,982
Yeah. You know, you don't have
to stay for the whole thing.

227
00:11:57,144 --> 00:11:59,903
Oh, no. I want to.
Sounds really interesting.

228
00:12:06,854 --> 00:12:07,733
Gentlemen.

229
00:12:16,469 --> 00:12:18,057
I'm just gonna sit down.

230
00:12:22,001 --> 00:12:23,634
So, is that your team?

231
00:12:23,975 --> 00:12:25,537
Actually, I don't need a team.

232
00:12:25,657 --> 00:12:27,554
I could easily defeat you
single-handedly,

233
00:12:27,748 --> 00:12:29,514
but the rules require four.

234
00:12:29,675 --> 00:12:32,672
So, may I introduce
the third-floor janitor,

235
00:12:32,792 --> 00:12:35,670
the lady from the lunch room,
and my Spanish is not good...

236
00:12:35,790 --> 00:12:37,604
either her son or her butcher.

237
00:12:38,688 --> 00:12:40,221
And what about your team?

238
00:12:40,341 --> 00:12:43,287
What rat have you recruited
to the S.S. Sinking Ship?

239
00:12:43,407 --> 00:12:44,501
Hello, Sheldon.

240
00:12:48,034 --> 00:12:49,077
Leslie Winkle.

241
00:12:49,473 --> 00:12:51,844
Yeah, Leslie Winkle.
The answer to the question,

242
00:12:51,964 --> 00:12:54,665
"Who made Sheldon Cooper cry
like a little girl?"

243
00:12:56,447 --> 00:13:00,003
Well, I'm polymerized tree sap,
and you're an inorganic adhesive.

244
00:13:00,123 --> 00:13:02,898
So whatever verbal projectile
you launch in my direction

245
00:13:03,018 --> 00:13:05,621
is reflected off of me,
returns on its original trajectory

246
00:13:05,741 --> 00:13:06,966
and adheres to you.

247
00:13:08,084 --> 00:13:09,317
Oh, ouch!

248
00:13:10,591 --> 00:13:13,226
Okay, if everyone could
please take your seats.

249
00:13:21,162 --> 00:13:22,583
Here's your T-shirt.

250
00:13:26,183 --> 00:13:27,136
PMS?

251
00:13:27,809 --> 00:13:29,435
It's a couple days early...

252
00:13:31,605 --> 00:13:34,371
It stands for "Perpetual Motion Squad."

253
00:13:34,757 --> 00:13:36,429
Of course.
What was I thinking?

254
00:13:38,414 --> 00:13:39,872
Good afternoon, everyone,

255
00:13:39,992 --> 00:13:42,910
and welcome to this year's Physics Bowl!

256
00:13:44,997 --> 00:13:48,468
Today's preliminary match
features two great teams.

257
00:13:49,009 --> 00:13:51,264
AA versus...

258
00:13:52,425 --> 00:13:53,365
PMS.

259
00:13:53,926 --> 00:13:55,488
All night long, y'all!

260
00:14:00,549 --> 00:14:04,484
Okay, well, let's jump right in.
First question for ten points:

261
00:14:04,830 --> 00:14:07,598
"What is the iso-spin singlet partner

262
00:14:07,718 --> 00:14:09,364
"of the pi-zero meson?"

263
00:14:10,443 --> 00:14:11,242
PMS?

264
00:14:11,403 --> 00:14:12,941
- The eta meson.
- Correct.

265
00:14:15,616 --> 00:14:17,438
- Formal protest.
- On what grounds?

266
00:14:17,558 --> 00:14:20,126
The Velcro on my wrist brace
caught on my shirt.

267
00:14:20,661 --> 00:14:23,615
Denied.
All right, for ten points,

268
00:14:23,832 --> 00:14:26,185
"What is the lightest element on Earth

269
00:14:26,305 --> 00:14:28,051
"with no stable isotope?"

270
00:14:28,392 --> 00:14:29,197
AA?

271
00:14:29,317 --> 00:14:31,219
And of course,
the answer is technetium.

272
00:14:31,381 --> 00:14:32,179
Terrific.

273
00:14:33,466 --> 00:14:34,515
Next question:

274
00:14:34,676 --> 00:14:37,227
"What is the force between
two uncharged plates

275
00:14:37,347 --> 00:14:39,561
"due to quantum vacuum fluctuations?"

276
00:14:40,037 --> 00:14:40,854
PMS?

277
00:14:41,016 --> 00:14:43,499
Sheldon can suck on...
the Casimir effect.

278
00:14:43,619 --> 00:14:44,497
Correct.

279
00:14:49,771 --> 00:14:53,210
How does a quantum computer
factor large numbers?

280
00:14:53,801 --> 00:14:54,619
PMS?

281
00:14:54,779 --> 00:14:56,493
- Shor's algorithm.
- Correct!

282
00:15:00,749 --> 00:15:03,627
4.1855 times ten
to the seventh ergs per calorie.

283
00:15:04,207 --> 00:15:05,852
Prevost's theory of exchanges.

284
00:15:06,449 --> 00:15:08,536
Lambda equals one
over pi r squared n.

285
00:15:09,031 --> 00:15:10,717
760 degrees Celsius...

286
00:15:10,879 --> 00:15:13,741
the approximate temperature
of the young lady in the front row.

287
00:15:14,966 --> 00:15:16,933
Mr. Wolowitz,
this is your second warning.

288
00:15:18,695 --> 00:15:19,835
A sigma particle.

289
00:15:20,233 --> 00:15:23,564
Yes, assuming the hypothetical planet
has a mass greater than the Earth.

290
00:15:23,726 --> 00:15:24,524
Correct!

291
00:15:30,014 --> 00:15:33,907
Ladies and gentlemen,
I hold in my hand the final question.

292
00:15:34,944 --> 00:15:38,077
The score now stands.
AA: 1,150,

293
00:15:38,197 --> 00:15:41,332
PMS: 1,175.

294
00:15:41,493 --> 00:15:44,984
So, for 100 points and the match,

295
00:15:45,104 --> 00:15:49,054
please turn your attention
to the formula on the screens.

296
00:15:49,667 --> 00:15:51,384
Solve the equation.

297
00:15:53,180 --> 00:15:54,320
Holy crap.

298
00:15:56,342 --> 00:15:57,599
What the hell is that?

299
00:15:57,949 --> 00:16:01,435
Looks like something
they found on the ship at Roswell.

300
00:16:01,596 --> 00:16:03,730
Come on. Think.
Leslie?

301
00:16:03,891 --> 00:16:06,774
It's not gonna work if you rush me.
You have to let me get there.

302
00:16:06,935 --> 00:16:09,276
You are never gonna
let that go, are you?

303
00:16:09,438 --> 00:16:10,653
Ten seconds.

304
00:16:14,806 --> 00:16:15,700
PMS?

305
00:16:17,768 --> 00:16:19,095
Sorry, I panicked.

306
00:16:20,698 --> 00:16:21,914
Then guess.

307
00:16:24,656 --> 00:16:25,591
Eight.

308
00:16:30,020 --> 00:16:31,459
.4

309
00:16:32,877 --> 00:16:34,786
I'm sorry, that's incorrect.

310
00:16:35,167 --> 00:16:35,971
AA,

311
00:16:36,685 --> 00:16:39,057
if you can answer correctly,
the match is yours.

312
00:16:42,162 --> 00:16:43,433
He doesn't have it.

313
00:16:44,049 --> 00:16:45,152
He's got squat.

314
00:16:54,664 --> 00:16:56,402
AA, I need your answer.

315
00:16:58,373 --> 00:17:01,206
The answer is minus eight pi alpha.

316
00:17:01,326 --> 00:17:03,163
Hang on a second.
That's not our answer.

317
00:17:03,325 --> 00:17:05,542
- What are you doing?
- Answering question.

318
00:17:05,662 --> 00:17:07,081
Winning Physics Bowl.

319
00:17:09,123 --> 00:17:11,081
How do you know anything
about physics?

320
00:17:11,201 --> 00:17:12,633
Here I am janitor.

321
00:17:12,753 --> 00:17:15,676
In former Soviet Union, I am physicist.

322
00:17:16,121 --> 00:17:17,970
Leningrad Politechnika.

323
00:17:18,461 --> 00:17:20,013
Go Polar Bears.

324
00:17:23,154 --> 00:17:24,792
That's a delightful little story,

325
00:17:24,912 --> 00:17:27,416
but our arrangement was that
you sit here and not say anything...

326
00:17:27,536 --> 00:17:30,489
- I answer the questions.
- You didn't answer question.

327
00:17:30,609 --> 00:17:33,631
Look, now, maybe you have
democracy now in your beloved Russia,

328
00:17:33,751 --> 00:17:36,611
but on this Physics Bowl team,
I rule with an iron fist.

329
00:17:38,517 --> 00:17:40,464
AA, I need your official answer.

330
00:17:40,654 --> 00:17:42,744
- It's not what he said.
- Then what is it?

331
00:17:42,957 --> 00:17:44,914
- I want a different question.
- You can't.

332
00:17:45,034 --> 00:17:46,457
- Formal protest.
- Denied.

333
00:17:46,618 --> 00:17:48,083
Informal protest.

334
00:17:48,832 --> 00:17:49,669
Denied.

335
00:17:50,621 --> 00:17:53,714
- I need your official answer.
- No. I decline to provide one.

336
00:17:53,876 --> 00:17:57,217
That's too bad because the answer
your teammate gave was correct.

337
00:17:58,703 --> 00:18:00,273
That's your opinion.

338
00:18:03,255 --> 00:18:05,852
- The winner of the match is...
- Hang on.

339
00:18:07,318 --> 00:18:10,987
Is proving that you are single-handedly
smarter than everyone else so important

340
00:18:11,107 --> 00:18:14,484
that you would rather lose by yourself
than win as part of a team?

341
00:18:16,169 --> 00:18:17,946
I don't understand the question.

342
00:18:19,408 --> 00:18:21,867
- Go ahead.
- The winner is PMS.

343
00:19:00,359 --> 00:19:02,110
Sorry, somebody's sitting there.

344
00:19:03,027 --> 00:19:03,827
Who?

345
00:19:04,153 --> 00:19:06,209
My Physics Bowl trophy.

346
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:12,126
That trophy is meaningless.
I forfeited, therefore you did not win.

347
00:19:12,286 --> 00:19:14,456
- I know someone who would disagree.
- Who?

348
00:19:14,576 --> 00:19:16,880
My Physics Bowl trophy.

349
00:19:18,136 --> 00:19:19,634
Leonard is so smart.

350
00:19:20,420 --> 00:19:23,095
- Sheldon who?
- All right, that is very immature.

351
00:19:23,256 --> 00:19:25,685
You're right. I'm sorry.
I'm not!

352
00:19:28,166 --> 00:19:30,597
- Okay, new contest.
- What are you doing?

353
00:19:30,896 --> 00:19:33,051
I am settling once and for all

354
00:19:33,171 --> 00:19:35,886
who is the smartest around here, okay?

355
00:19:36,006 --> 00:19:37,314
- Ready?
- Absolutely.

356
00:19:37,434 --> 00:19:38,486
Bring it on.

357
00:19:39,118 --> 00:19:41,735
"Marsha, Jan and Cindy
were the three daughters

358
00:19:41,855 --> 00:19:43,283
"in what TV family?"

359
00:19:47,762 --> 00:19:48,829
<i>The Brady Bunch.</i>

360
00:19:51,743 --> 00:19:55,587
"Sammy Hagar replaced David Lee Roth
as the lead singer in what group?"

361
00:20:00,486 --> 00:20:01,626
<i>The Brady Bunch?</i>

362
00:20:04,467 --> 00:20:05,430
<i>Van Halen.</i>

363
00:20:06,966 --> 00:20:09,601
"Madonna was married
to this Ridgemont High alum."

364
00:20:11,947 --> 00:20:14,111
Oh, my God! Sean Penn!

365
00:20:14,307 --> 00:20:16,244
How do you know these things?

366
00:20:17,083 --> 00:20:19,943
I go outside,
and I talk to people.

367
00:20:21,806 --> 00:20:24,337
Okay, here.
"What actor holds the record

368
00:20:24,457 --> 00:20:26,993
"for being named <i>People Magazine</i>'s
Sexiest Man Alive?"

369
00:20:27,452 --> 00:20:28,995
- William Shatner.
- Wait.

370
00:20:31,800 --> 00:20:33,389
I don't think it's Shatner.

371
00:20:33,744 --> 00:20:35,651
Then it's got to be Patrick Stewart.

372
00:20:39,754 --> 00:20:41,006
Formal protest.

373
00:20:43,167 --> 00:20:45,428
"Singer who sang,
<i>Oops, I Did It Again</i>?"

374
00:20:53,649 --> 00:20:56,397
"Tweetie Bird tought
he taw a what?"

375
00:21:00,471 --> 00:21:01,461
Romulan.

376
00:21:05,910 --> 00:21:06,844
Yes.

377
00:21:06,964 --> 00:21:09,199
He tought he taw a Romulan.

